From: Pyra kernel work <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: ti: aess: size the ping-pong buffer from the firmware

Playing real audio through the AESS produced pulsing noise. A steady sine
sounded fine, which is why this survived so long: a sine is identical
whether you play it once or replay one buffer forever.

omap_aess_set_ping_pong_buffer() hands the firmware a base address from
pp->buf_addr[], cycling buf_id_next over FOUR entries laid out at
offset + i * period_bytes. So the window in use spans 4 * period_bytes --
not the two that "ping-pong" suggests, and not the two that the n_buffers=2
argument sets up in base_address[].

The driver advertised period_bytes_max of 24 KiB, so ALSA picked a
12288-byte period and the window became 49152 bytes. The firmware reserves
24576 for D_PING, so buf_addr[2] and [3] landed at 0xA000 and 0xD000 --
past the region, in the middle of other DMEM. The DSP replayed the first
buffers while the writes corrupted what followed.

Cap a period at a quarter of the region, read from the firmware's own map
at open() so a different firmware cannot silently reintroduce this.
---
--- a/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c
@@ -74,10 +74,27 @@
 	.formats		= SNDRV_PCM_FMTBIT_S16_LE |
 				  SNDRV_PCM_FMTBIT_S32_LE,
 	.period_bytes_min	= 4 * 1024,
-	.period_bytes_max	= 24 * 1024,
+	/*
+	 * A period may be at most a quarter of the DMEM ping-pong region, and
+	 * the real limit is computed from the firmware map in
+	 * omap_aess_pcm_open() -- these are only the fallback ceilings.
+	 *
+	 * omap_aess_set_ping_pong_buffer() hands the firmware a base address
+	 * from pp->buf_addr[], cycling buf_id_next over FOUR entries laid out
+	 * at offset + i * period_bytes. So the window actually in use spans
+	 * 4 * period_bytes, not the two that "ping-pong" suggests and not the
+	 * two that the n_buffers=2 argument sets up in base_address[].
+	 *
+	 * With the 24576-byte region this firmware reserves, the old 24 KiB
+	 * ceiling let ALSA pick a 12288-byte period, so buf_addr[2] and [3]
+	 * landed at 0xA000 and 0xD000 -- past the region, in the middle of
+	 * other DMEM. The DSP replayed the first buffers and the writes
+	 * corrupted whatever followed. It sounded like pulsing noise.
+	 */
+	.period_bytes_max	= 6 * 1024,
 	.periods_min		= 4,
 	.periods_max		= 4,
-	.buffer_bytes_max	= 24 * 1024 * 2,
+	.buffer_bytes_max	= 6 * 1024 * 4,
 };
 
 static int aess_save_context(struct omap_aess *aess)
@@ -303,8 +320,17 @@
 
 	dev_dbg(dai->dev, "%s: %s\n", __func__, dai->name);
 
-	if (dai->id == OMAP_AESS_DAI_MM1_LP)
-		snd_soc_set_runtime_hwparams(substream, &omap_aess_hardware);
+	if (dai->id == OMAP_AESS_DAI_MM1_LP) {
+		struct snd_pcm_hardware hw = omap_aess_hardware;
+		u32 ping = aess->fw_info.map[OMAP_AESS_DMEM_PING_ID].bytes;
+
+		/* Four rotating buffers have to fit the reserved region. */
+		if (ping / 4 < hw.period_bytes_max)
+			hw.period_bytes_max = ping / 4;
+		hw.buffer_bytes_max = hw.period_bytes_max * 4;
+
+		snd_soc_set_runtime_hwparams(substream, &hw);
+	}
 
 	pm_runtime_get_sync(aess->dev);
 
